1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,720
And what does that do to the value of the econ coefficient?

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:07,120
What does it do to it?

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:08,120
It makes it big.

4
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:09,680
Because the whole idea of regression

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,440
is that you are trying to compare people

6
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:15,760
who are alike in every other way, but different in one thing.

7
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:18,760
So if economists tend to be in the group that's especially

8
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:22,440
worried about downsizing, except because they're economists,

9
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:23,960
and only because they're economists,

10
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:25,720
they have a very different view, then

11
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:31,880
that actually amplifies the effect of economic training.

12
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:32,960
So other questions?

13
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:33,760
Yeah, Ash?

14
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:35,840
Just on that notion of subjective

15
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,240
versus objective probability, do you know,

16
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:39,720
this is a weird question, but do you

17
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:41,320
know if there's any literature that

18
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:45,080
relates objective probability to cardinal probability

19
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,520
and subjective probability to ordinal probability?

20
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:49,800
So I don't know anything about ordinal

21
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:50,960
versus cardinal probability.

22
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:53,200
It's a very small topic.

23
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:57,960
Sounds like it is a topic, but it's not a big topic.

24
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:01,240
So again, what an ordinal probability would be like?

25
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,200
If you say, well, I can say that one probability is

26
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,200
more likely than another, but I can't tell you

27
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,000
how much more likely?

28
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,520
All right, so I guess you can imagine a,

29
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,600
I'm sure a mathematician could design that system,

30
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:15,440
but it would be hard to explain, at least to me.

31
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:17,040
Certainly hard for me to explain.

32
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:18,800
It would also be hard to explain it to me,

33
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,480
because I would sit there saying, what?

34
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,360
No!

35
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:25,360
What?

36
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:28,280
All right, so moving on then.

37
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:30,840
All right, now, we are back in week two.

38
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:34,320
We already did a very general model of economic action

39
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:37,720
under uncertainty, which is the Erdo-de-Bruin model.

40
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:40,800
You can remember the complete contingent claims market

41
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,760
model, so that is a model of choice under uncertainty.

42
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:45,760
But complete contingent claims markets

43
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,040
is a highly unrealistic assumption.

44
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:50,880
There are most of the time, you can't go and say, look,

45
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:53,360
I want to go and get a computer, but I only

46
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:57,720
want it if the processing speed of computer chips

47
00:01:57,720 --> 00:01:59,640
grows at a slower rate than this.

48
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:02,200
How much will you charge me for that computer?

49
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:04,400
And normally, you'll say, we just won't.

50
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,720
No, leave our store.

51
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:09,000
We don't want to go and start making weird contracts

52
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,960
with every crackpot who comes off the street

53
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:12,160
and want something different.

54
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:14,840
Say, look, we got three options here.

55
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:18,320
We got this computer, that computer, and the other.

56
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:21,640
But I want 2 thirds of the first one and 1 third of the second

57
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:22,520
one.

58
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,680
We can't say no.

59
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,800
All right, so is there any other approach

60
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:29,960
to economic action under uncertainty?

61
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:32,320
And the answer, of course, is yes.

62
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,960
There's a very general theory of economic action

63
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:53,760
under uncertainty, and its name is associated.

64
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,480
Partly, I teach this because I've

65
00:02:55,480 --> 00:02:58,600
had a horrible experience at some economic conferences,

66
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:00,800
and I want to make sure that none of you

67
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:02,000
are ever part of the problem.

68
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,960
So you'll all be part of the solution.

69
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:06,000
So I've been at several economics conferences

70
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,440
where economics professors get in front of an audience,

71
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:12,480
and I've literally heard more than one person say,

72
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:16,040
well, as we all know, economics assumes perfect information.

73
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:17,040
Oh, god.

74
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:18,920
And then I say, especially then, I look around

75
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:20,760
and I see some journalists in the room.

76
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:23,440
I'm like, what would any decent journalist be thinking?

77
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:24,480
Wait a second.

78
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:27,280
So you're a professor of a subject, right?

79
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:30,200
And your subject assumes perfect information.

80
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:31,640
You just said so.

81
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:33,200
And I ought to trust you.

82
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,120
This would be a very basic thing for you to get wrong.

83
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:40,840
And when in the real world do we ever have perfect information?

84
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:41,560
Never.

85
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:44,720
So when does your subject describe the world?

86
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:45,640
Never.

87
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:47,160
So why do you exist?

88
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:49,120
What is the point of this field?

89
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:51,080
So to me, whenever I hear economists say this,

90
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:53,720
I do want to curl up to a ball and either that

91
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:54,760
or snap my arm up.

92
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:56,720
Hey!

93
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:57,600
Wrong, professor!

94
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:03,080
So of course, it is absolutely untrue

95
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,960
to say that economics assumes perfect information.

96
00:04:05,960 --> 00:04:08,400
There are some economic models with perfect information,

97
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:11,520
but it doesn't mean the subject assumes perfect information.

98
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:12,320
Of course not.

99
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:13,880
That is ridiculous.

100
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:16,120
There are multiple Nobel Prizes for economics

101
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:18,480
with under uncertainty.

102
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:21,240
So how could they possibly say something so wrong

103
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:23,080
and don't know?

104
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:25,160
Can't figure out what would possess a person

105
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:28,400
to say something so ridiculous when they know better.

106
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:33,040
I mean, it's kind of like, what about Akerlof?

107
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:34,400
It's like, what do you mean?

108
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,120
Well, he gave us some models of economic action

109
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,000
under uncertainty, so what you said was wrong.

110
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,920
Oh, yeah.

111
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,920
Oh, yeah, economics assumes it, but some famous economists

112
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,320
don't assume it.

113
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:49,320
Oh, yeah, they develop poor careers doing it.

114
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:53,160
So search theory is one very general model

115
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,840
of economic action under uncertainty.

116
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:59,880
So this came out, so originally, it was a stigma.

117
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:03,000
And just for history of thought, this came out a year or two

118
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,880
before Israel Kirzner did anything on entrepreneurship.

119
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:11,640
So this is not a case where a Chicago economist was just

120
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:13,840
formalizing what an Austrian had said.

121
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:15,360
Far from it.

122
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,920
So it's more a case of possibly when an Austrian eyes

123
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:21,520
what a Chicago economist had said.

124
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:22,960
Any case.

125
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,760
So stringly general theory.

126
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:27,800
It has the following basic assumptions.

127
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:31,720
So first one we can call these just the assumption of effort,

128
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:36,000
just as we put more time and effort into searching,

129
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:37,600
increase your probability of finding

130
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:39,440
whatever you're looking for.

131
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:41,360
Pretty simple point.

132
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:44,480
The next assumption, which is not absolutely theoretically

133
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:47,320
vital, but is important in the real world,

134
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:50,600
is to say that searching ability differs between people.

135
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:53,000
It's like my mom can find something in her refrigerator

136
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:54,480
that I could not find.

137
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:57,800
At least she could take far less time for her to find something

138
00:05:57,800 --> 00:05:58,800
than I would take leave.

139
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:01,400
So she has superior ability to search her own refrigerator

140
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:02,840
than I do.

141
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:08,160
And then third assumption, rational expectations.

142
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:10,480
So this can be relaxed, of course.

143
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:13,080
By standard model search theory, rational expectations

144
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:14,200
is built in.

145
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:16,760
So when people are trading off between the amount of time

146
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:18,920
that they're going to put in studying or putting in

147
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:27,040
searching, they are on average making the correct choice.

148
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:28,800
And the main conclusion is that people

149
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:32,120
keep searching until the expected marginal benefit

150
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:35,520
of search is equal to the expected marginal cost.

151
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:38,680
You can also add in a risk aversion adjustment if you want.

152
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,440
That's the basic idea.

153
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,400
So we say things like, I'm looking for my socks.

154
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:44,680
First of all, if I put more effort

155
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:47,280
in trying to find my socks, I'm more likely to find my socks.

156
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:47,840
I don't know.

157
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:49,840
I guess I'm in the plane.

158
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:52,400
I was sitting next to a woman who lost an earring at the end.

159
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:53,600
And then she conscripted my daughter

160
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:55,920
to help with the search on the floor of the plane.

161
00:06:55,920 --> 00:06:58,120
She'd been nice, so we were perfectly happy to do it.

162
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:00,160
So putting more effort.

163
00:07:00,160 --> 00:07:02,320
So you could just hope that you step on the earring

164
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:03,820
and you'll find it, but she did not.

165
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:05,640
So she's getting on her hands and knees

166
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:08,640
and enlisting a small child to go and do the search,

167
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:09,760
and help her.

168
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:11,160
And then searching ability.

169
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:12,880
So while my daughter's trying very hard,

170
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:14,840
I think her actual ability to find her earring

171
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:15,960
is pretty low.

172
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:17,640
She might not know what an earring is.

173
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:20,080
We want to be a little hazy on her anyway.

174
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:21,880
And then finally, rationalizations.

175
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:24,280
So she was saying, well, maybe it's just time to give up.

176
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:26,360
Oh, no, there it is.

177
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:28,680
But certainly while she was stuck on the plane,

178
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:30,440
there was very little to lose in putting some more time

179
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:31,960
into searching for this earring.

180
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:33,040
I think she cared about it enough

181
00:07:33,040 --> 00:07:34,960
that she was going to wait for everyone to go and leave,

182
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:36,560
and then do a more thorough search

183
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:38,160
without other bodies around.

184
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:41,380
But when she finally saw it right next to one of the screws

185
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:43,360
that screws one of the seats down,

186
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,360
it was very, very well hidden.

187
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:46,800
But she kept saying, I know it's got to be

188
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,160
around here somewhere.

189
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:50,200
That sounds like rationalizations.

190
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:51,920
It's got to be around here somewhere.

191
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:53,560
Earrings don't just disappear.

192
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:54,760
They don't run around.

193
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:56,480
They don't bounce that far.

194
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:59,000
They bounce once or twice.

195
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,840
Now, there are endless applications of search theory.

196
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,400
So there's things like doing R&D,

197
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:08,240
so you're trying to find a cure for AIDS, for example.

198
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:08,960
So effort.

199
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:10,720
You're more likely to find a cure for AIDS

200
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:12,800
if you actually start hiring some AIDS researchers

201
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:15,400
and giving them some money and running experiments

202
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:17,080
and testing things.

203
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:20,000
So of course, it's possible that someone will just get lucky.

204
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,120
Like the story of this discovery of penicillin.

205
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:24,840
Someone just accidentally dropped two things together.

206
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:26,560
You've got chocolate in my peanut butter.

207
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,360
Now we have a cure for a bunch of bacterial infections.

208
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,320
And then, of course, the difference is ability.

209
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,440
So you could hire a bunch of people who've never done AIDS

210
00:08:34,440 --> 00:08:36,520
research or medical research of any kind.

211
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:38,640
But you have an idea that some people would just

212
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:40,080
be better at this.

213
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:43,240
So who is more likely to cure AIDS?

214
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:47,640
Me or a random AIDS researcher.

215
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:48,840
It's possible I will do it.

216
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:51,600
But it seems a lot more likely that an AIDS researcher will

217
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:53,400
do it because they know what's going on.

218
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,800
And then, again, rationalizations.

219
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:57,200
You have an idea about the odds.

220
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:01,680
On average, you have correct beliefs about the odds

221
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:03,960
of being able to or there were different probabilities

222
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:06,800
of success and your ability to enforce those odds.

223
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:08,840
Of course, you knew this for hunting and fishing,

224
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:11,120
which were the main human economic activities

225
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:13,880
for tens of thousands of years.

226
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:16,280
So if you go and leave your village

227
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:17,860
and start looking around for animals,

228
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:19,520
you're more likely to find an animal.

229
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:21,200
And then there's differences in ability.

230
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:22,960
There are the trained hunters who

231
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:24,600
have honed their skills for decades.

232
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:26,520
And then there are the people who don't really

233
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:27,600
know what they're doing.

234
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,800
And it's possible that the novice will kill the deer.

235
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:32,840
But it's much more likely that the trained person who

236
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:37,120
is at just the right combination of age and experience

237
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:39,080
will be successful.

238
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:42,080
So Robin is really interested in foragers.

239
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:46,240
So I think your maximum foraging ability

240
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:48,360
is when you're about 30 to 35.

241
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,280
It's when you've had a lot of experience,

242
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:52,240
but your body still works pretty well.

243
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:54,740
And then, of course, younger people, their bodies work well,

244
00:09:54,740 --> 00:09:56,400
but they don't know what they're doing.

245
00:09:56,400 --> 00:09:58,160
And older people know what they're doing,

246
00:09:58,160 --> 00:10:02,160
but their bodies don't work very well anymore.

247
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:04,200
And then you can find the rationalizations.

248
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:06,320
You have this idea, well, where are we, in fact,

249
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,800
likely to go and find deer?

250
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:08,960
Where do they usually go?

251
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:10,720
What is the typical patterns?

252
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:12,680
What is a high-uncompetent probability?

253
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:14,040
So on.

254
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:15,720
There could be prospecting for gold.

255
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:17,880
You're more likely to find gold if you put in some effort

256
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:18,520
into finding it.

257
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:19,600
But that's no guarantee.

258
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:22,360
But nevertheless, some people have greater ability

259
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:23,880
to find gold than other people.

260
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:25,760
And then, of course, rationalizations.

261
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:26,640
If I told you that I was just going

262
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:31,400
to spend tomorrow looking around for gold,

263
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:34,040
you would not have much hope that I would succeed, would you?

264
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:36,320
Nor should you have such hope.

265
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,800
You're unlikely to succeed.

266
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:41,720
Then it could be looking for investment opportunities.

267
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:43,360
So you can think about an entrepreneur

